From 776d8bf6682e2f999df15bd291b32f7d941367c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 01:50:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 23/39] um: cheaper fork: inherited accessed bit, mprotect
 sync, write-fault-around

A Python fork()+waitpid() took about 105 guest page faults, each a host
signal plus a stub handoff (30-50us on an S24):

- fork() gave the child old PTEs, which are mapped without access here, so
  every inherited page faulted on first touch. arch_fork_pte_mkold() lets
  the child keep the parent's accessed bit (fork_young=).
- A PTE change that keeps the page (write-protect at fork, write access
  back, young/dirty) is now marked _PAGE_PROTONLY and synced with
  STUB_SYSCALL_MPROTECT, which merges over pages that are not adjacent in
  physmem (tlb_protect=).
- A write fault on a page that fork() write-protected also gives write
  access back to up to 64 neighbours on each side that are dirty and no
  longer shared (wp_around=).

The stub's syscall switch grew a case; build stub.o with -fno-jump-tables,
the table would live outside the one page that is mapped.

S24, Python 3.13 fork+waitpid: 8.4 -> 6.5 ms in one A/B boot, faults per
fork 105 -> 58.

