From 0a06c3bfa9cac217a04ccfa0033a51a89d263bff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 06:44:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 31/39] um/arm64: leave FP registers in the stub frame across
 syscalls

get_stub_state and set_stub_state copied the 528-byte FPSIMD record on
every trap: reading what the stub had just written on another core, then
writing it back for the stub to read again. On a Neoverse N1 VM that
cache-line traffic was half of a guest syscall.

A syscall never changes FP state, so for a single-threaded mm leave the
registers in the frame and record where they are (regs->fp_src). Anything
that reads or writes regs->fp syncs first: signal frames, the FP regset
(ptrace, core dumps), fork, exec and the ptrace path. Creating a thread
goes through copy_thread, which syncs, so no other task can overwrite the
frame of a lazy one. fp_lazy=0 (core_param) turns it off.

A1 same-boot A/B: getppid 2.0-2.9 -> 1.25us, pipe 4.3-6.2 -> 2.8us.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01B3kysVLGNgK27oVbqXzFEn
