From 4852e09e4bf9f135b76b94709daf260702c3da04 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 03:24:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 33/39] um: give each guest thread a host thread of its own in
 the stub

Guest threads of one mm took turns on the stub's single host thread,
so a multithreaded program ran on one CPU and every switch between its
threads was a sleep and a wakeup (two threads making syscalls: 594us
each instead of 0.17us on an A1 host).

Each further guest thread (CLONE_VM without CLONE_VFORK) now gets a host
thread of its own, made by the parent's host thread with
STUB_SYSCALL_THREAD, with its own data pages at a fixed slot between
TASK_SIZE and the stub and its own socket. The signal handler finds the
data pages from sp. Host threads are kept for reuse until the stub
goes. Syncs of such an mm are flushed before the locks go, fork syncs
the parent first, and the timer and IPIs signal the right host thread.
The syscall fast path enters through the main thread's data pages; with
more host threads it asks gettid which ones are its own.
stub_threads=0 turns it off.

A1, 2 vCPUs, % of native: xz -T2 20 -> 83, sort --parallel=2 13 -> 68,
python thread pool 31 -> 75, git status 0.5 -> 18; two computing
threads now get 1.96x of one.

